---
title: System Upgrade
order: 3
description: -Syu, partial upgrade risks, downgrading
tags: [pacman, upgrade, update, downgrade, syu]
---

System upgrades and dealing with version issues.

@code bash
# ---- full system upgrade ----

pacman -Syu                               # sync databases + upgrade all
pacman -Syuu                              # allow downgrading packages too

# check what would be upgraded (dry run)
checkupdates                              # safe, doesn't touch sync db
# install: pacman -S pacman-contrib

@text
CRITICAL: Never run `pacman -Sy` without `-u`. Syncing the database
without upgrading causes a partial upgrade, which can break the system.
Libraries may be updated in the database but not on disk, causing
applications to fail.

Unsafe operations:
- `pacman -Sy package` -- installs against new database, old libraries
- `pacman -Sy` then later `pacman -S package` -- same problem

Safe operations:
- `pacman -Syu` -- full upgrade
- `pacman -Syu package` -- upgrade everything + install package

@code bash
# ---- downgrading packages ----

# from cache (if old version is still cached)
pacman -U /var/cache/pacman/pkg/package-oldversion-x86_64.pkg.tar.zst

# list cached versions
ls /var/cache/pacman/pkg/package-*

# from Arch Linux Archive (ALA)
# https://archive.archlinux.org/packages/p/package/
pacman -U https://archive.archlinux.org/packages/p/package/package-oldversion-x86_64.pkg.tar.zst

@code bash
# ---- hold/ignore packages ----

# skip specific packages during upgrade
pacman -Syu --ignore package1,package2

# permanent ignore in /etc/pacman.conf
# IgnorePkg = package1 package2
# IgnoreGroup = groupname

@code bash
# ---- handling .pacnew and .pacsave files ----

# find all pacnew/pacsave files
find /etc -name "*.pacnew" -o -name "*.pacsave" 2>/dev/null
pacdiff                                   # interactive merge tool (pacman-contrib)

# manually compare and merge
diff /etc/config.conf /etc/config.conf.pacnew
vimdiff /etc/config.conf /etc/config.conf.pacnew

@text
.pacnew files appear when pacman upgrades a package and the config file
on disk has been modified by the user. The new default config is saved
as .pacnew. You must manually merge changes.

.pacsave files appear when a package is removed and the config file was
modified. The modified config is saved as .pacsave.

@code bash
# ---- troubleshooting upgrades ----

# if keyring is out of date
pacman -Sy archlinux-keyring artix-keyring && pacman -Su

# if database is locked
rm /var/lib/pacman/db.lck                 # only if no pacman is running

# if file conflicts during upgrade
pacman -Syu --overwrite '/path/to/file'

# check for broken packages
pacman -Qk                                # check all package files
pacman -Qk package                        # check specific package
pacman -Qkk package                       # more thorough check (permissions, etc.)
